<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
        There is no INTERNET permission here and there never will be. It is stated
        to users as a fact their phone enforces, so it is checked rather than
        trusted: verifyNoInternetPermission fails the build if the merged manifest
        ever contains one, whether it came from here or from a dependency.

        WorkManager declares ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE so that callers can schedule work
        behind a network constraint. This app schedules two pieces of work, a Pulse
        reminder and a widget refresh, and neither has a network constraint because
        neither could use one. The permission is removed rather than shipped, since
        a permission the app cannot make use of still shows up in the app info
        screen a person is invited to go and check.

        **Phase 6 is the first real caller, and the removal was checked against the
        library rather than assumed.** In work-runtime 2.11.2,
        WorkConstraintsTracker.track filters its controllers by
        ConstraintController.hasConstraint for the spec it is given, so a request
        with no constraints never reaches the network tracker and never starts it.
        Everything that does touch ConnectivityManager, the state read and the
        default network callback registration, catches SecurityException already. A
        constraint free daily reminder is therefore safe without the permission.
        **Adding any constraint to a work request in this app means putting the
        permission back**, which would be a change to what the privacy policy invites
        people to verify, so it is a decision for the owner and not a build fix.

        The three WorkManager permissions this app does ship are WAKE_LOCK,
        RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED and FOREGROUND_SERVICE. None is a network permission
        and verifyNoInternetPermission passes on both variants. MASTER_BUILD_PROMPT
        section 18 puts every permission beyond notifications out of scope for v1,
        which these are, and docs/BUILD_STATE.md records the question as the
        builder's call. Phase 6 does not settle it: it uses WorkManager because
        section 3 and 12.1 both require it for this reminder, and reports what the
        merged manifest gains.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE"
        tools:node="remove" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
        The focus session Live Update, MASTER_BUILD_PROMPT 14b.6 and design-v3.md
        11.4. `Notification.ProgressStyle` is a promoted ongoing notification and
        the platform gates promotion on this permission.

        **It is a notifications permission and not a network one**, which is what
        keeps it inside what MASTER_BUILD_PROMPT 18 allows and what keeps
        verifyNoInternetPermission green on both variants: that gate matches five
        exact permission names, INTERNET, ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE, ACCESS_WIFI_STATE,
        CHANGE_NETWORK_STATE and CHANGE_WIFI_STATE, and this is none of them.

        A normal permission, so it is granted at install and shows no prompt. It
        grants nothing beyond asking the system to promote a notification the app is
        already allowed to post, and the system may still refuse:
        NotificationManagerCompat.canPostPromotedNotifications is checked before
        every post and a false answer degrades to the ongoing notification with no
        word to the user, per design-v3.md 11.4.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_PROMOTED_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
        Required by design-v3.md section 9, which specifies sixteen haptic events.
        A normal permission: it is granted at install, shows no prompt, and gives
        access to nothing but the vibrator on this device.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.VIBRATE" />

    <application
        android:name=".ClarityApp"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="${appLabel}"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="false"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.ClarityNow"
        tools:targetApi="31">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|keyboardHidden|uiMode"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.ClarityNow"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
                The three static app shortcuts on a long press of the icon.
                MASTER_BUILD_PROMPT 13.5, design-v3.md 12.4, issue #40.

                It sits on this activity because the platform reads this meta-data from
                the activity that carries MAIN and LAUNCHER, and there is exactly one.
                The resource says what the three are and why none of them is dynamic.
            -->
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.app.shortcuts"
                android:resource="@xml/shortcuts" />
        </activity>

        <!--
            The two Live Update actions, `Add 10 min` and `End`, and the dismissal of
            the ongoing notification. Addendum 01 5c requires both actions to work
            without opening the app, so they arrive here rather than at an Activity.

            **Not exported.** Every intent that reaches it is built by this app with
            an explicit component, so there is no filter to match and nothing outside
            the app can start a session, end one or add time to one.

            It writes through ClarityRepository like every other writer in the app,
            per CLAUDE.md rule on the single write path. It is not a second one.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".notifications.FocusActionReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!--
            The three widgets built in this phase, Quick Capture, Focus Countdown and
            Rhythm. MASTER_BUILD_PROMPT 13.3 and design-v3.md 12.2.

            **Exported, and it has to be.** An app widget provider is a broadcast
            receiver the platform sends APPWIDGET_UPDATE to on behalf of the launcher,
            so a provider that is not exported is a widget that never draws. Each one
            handles that one action and nothing else, carries no data, and the class
            behind it reads a snapshot and renders it.

            The label is what the widget picker shows. The metadata resource is the size,
            the resize rules and the picker description, one file per widget in res/xml.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".widget.QuickCaptureWidgetReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/widget_quick_capture_label">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/widget_quick_capture" />
        </receiver>

        <receiver
            android:name=".widget.FocusCountdownWidgetReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/widget_focus_countdown_label">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/widget_focus_countdown" />
        </receiver>

        <receiver
            android:name=".widget.RhythmWidgetReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/widget_rhythm_label">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/widget_rhythm" />
        </receiver>

        <!--
            The three area widgets, Next Up, First Step and All Areas.
            MASTER_BUILD_PROMPT 13.3 and design-v3.md 12.2. Exported for the reason the
            three above are: a provider the platform cannot broadcast to never draws.

            Each names a configuration screen and each marks it optional, so a widget is
            placed and drawn immediately with the automatic area and configured
            afterward if the person wants to pin one. res/xml/widget_next_up.xml says
            why at more length.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".widget.NextUpWidgetReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/widget_next_up_label">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/widget_next_up" />
        </receiver>

        <receiver
            android:name=".widget.FirstStepWidgetReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/widget_first_step_label">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/widget_first_step" />
        </receiver>

        <receiver
            android:name=".widget.AllAreasWidgetReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/widget_all_areas_label">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/widget_all_areas" />
        </receiver>

        <!--
            The screen that points one placed widget at one area.

            **Exported, because the launcher starts it**, which is how a configuration
            activity is reached at all. It is given an app widget id and nothing else,
            and everything it can do is write which area that one widget shows. An app
            widget id it does not own resolves to no widget and the screen closes.

            Excluded from recents: it is a modal helper opened from a home screen, and a
            person swiping through their recent apps should not find it sitting there
            after they have chosen an area.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".widget.WidgetConfigurationActivity"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.ClarityNow">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_CONFIGURE" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
            The quick settings tile that starts or ends a focus session from the shade.
            MASTER_BUILD_PROMPT 13.5, design-v3.md 12.4, issue #41.

            **Exported, and guarded by a permission only the system holds.** A tile
            service is bound by System UI, so it has to be exported to be bound at all,
            and `BIND_QUICK_SETTINGS_TILE` is what makes that the only binder. It is a
            permission required **of** the caller rather than one this app asks for, so
            it adds nothing to the merged manifest's uses-permission list.

            The icon is `timer`, from design-v3.md section 7, and it is the same in both
            states: the label says which of the two things a tap does and the tile's own
            active state says which one is true, so a third signal would be one more
            thing that can disagree with the other two.

            The label here is the fixed name in the tile picker, where the tile is being
            chosen rather than used. The live labels are set in the service.

            **No ACTIVE_TILE meta-data**, so this is an ordinary tile: the system tells
            it when the shade opens and it reads the running session then. An active
            tile would put the app in charge of saying when to redraw, which would mean
            waking to update something nobody is looking at.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".tile.FocusTileService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:icon="@drawable/ic_timer"
            android:label="@string/tile_focus_name"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_QUICK_SETTINGS_TILE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.quicksettings.action.QS_TILE" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>
    </application>
</manifest>
